        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "f92055bc-64b9-577a-8ecc-0a4432afab9b",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Lincoln Steffens, Monique Wittig, Doc Ames\n- Works: McClure's, \"Tweed Days in St. Louis\", \"The Shame of the Cities\"\n- Places: New York, St. Louis, Minneapolis\n- Topics: Feminist theory, gender roles, organized crime\n\nFacts:\n- Lincoln Steffens was born on April 6, 1866, and he was a New York reporter.\n- In 1903, organized crime in Minneapolis attracted national attention when Doc Ames was exposed by Lincoln Steffens in \"The Shame of the Cities\".\n- Lincoln Steffens launched a series of articles in McClure's called \"Tweed Days in St. Louis\", and those articles were later published together as the book \"The Shame of the Cities\".\n- Monique Wittig was born on July 13, 1935, and she was a French author and feminist theorist.\n- Monique Wittig wrote about overcoming socially enforced gender roles and coined the phrase \"heterosexual contract\".\n- Monique Wittig died on January 3, 2003.\n- Lincoln Steffens died on August 9, 1936, and Doc Ames lived from 1842 to 1911.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "166e40ce-8be8-58e6-8c69-5cde88c6352d",
        "text": "Page: Lincoln Steffens. Sentence: Lincoln Joseph Steffens (April 6, 1866 – August 9, 1936) was a New York reporter who launched a series of articles in \"McClure's\", called \"Tweed Days in St. Louis\", that would later be published together in a book titled \"The Shame of the Cities\".\nPage: Monique Wittig. Sentence: Monique Wittig (] ; July 13, 1935 – January 3, 2003) was a French author and feminist theorist who wrote about overcoming socially enforced gender roles and who coined the phrase \"heterosexual contract\".\nPage: Organized crime in Minneapolis. Sentence: Organized crime in Minneapolis first attracted national attention in 1903, when thug and mayor Doc Ames (1842-1911) was exposed by Lincoln Steffens in the book \"The Shame of the Cities\".",
